==Sanskrit==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Indo-European|óykos|lang=sa|one, single}}. Cognates include Old Latin {{term|oinos|lang=la}} (Classical Latin {{term|unus|ūnus|lang=la}}), Ancient Greek {{term|sc=polytonic|οἶος|tr=oios|lang=grc}} and Old English {{term|an|ān|lang=ang}} (English {{term|one|lang=en}} and {{term|an|lang=en}}).

===Numeral===
{{head|sa|numeral|sc=Deva|tr=éka}}

# [[one]]

===Adjective===
{{head|sa|adjective|sc=Deva|tr=éka}}

# (with and without {{term|sc=Deva|tr=eva|एव|lang=sa}}) [[alone]], [[solitary]], [[single]], happening only once, that one only
# the [[same]], one and the same, [[identical]]
# one of two or many
# single of its kind, [[unique]], [[singular]], [[chief]], pre-eminent, [[excellent]]
# [[sincere]], [[truthful]]
# [[little]], [[small]]
# (sometimes used as an indefinite article), [[a]], [[an]]

===Noun===
{{sa-noun|tr=éka|g=m}}

# name of a teacher
# name of a son of {{term|sc=Deva|रय|tr=Raya|lang=sa}}

===Noun===
{{sa-noun|tr=éka|g=n}}

# [[unity]], a [[unit]] (at the end of a compound)

